WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:04.033
Nobody approves their own work.
Now, how fast you let the work move.

00:00:04.033 --> 00:00:07.167
Go live at your own speed:
every message held as a draft,

00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:10.200
one engine opened at a time,
hard limits around each.

00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:11.633
By the end you'll know the two

00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:13.767
safety switches and the
limits that hold work.

00:00:13.767 --> 00:00:16.033
You'll also know how much
autonomy an agent gets,

00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:18.567
and the check to run before
any engine goes live.

00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:20.533
Leo turns on outreach with no limits.

00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:23.133
Within an hour the whole list
has been emailed in one burst,

00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:24.433
in the middle of the night, their time.

00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:26.567
Nothing broke, and nothing
was in the way either.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.000
First, Dry Mode, the
organization wide switch.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.100
While it's on, every
external effect, like a send,

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.800
becomes a logged draft and doesn't
run. A banner shows on every page.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.500
In the console: Settings, Safety.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.933
Or ask your agent: "Is Dry Mode
on, and which engines are live".

00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:42.400
It can read the state, but
only a person can change it.

00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:45.700
Second, each engine has
its own mode, Dry or Live.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.033
The stricter of the two always applies.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.833
Dry doesn't mean invisible, because
every draft lands in the actions ledger,

00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:54.133
per engine, with the gate
that applied and the outcome.

00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:57.433
In the console: Operate,
Actions, filtered by engine.

00:00:57.433 --> 00:00:59.800
So during a dry period you judge
what GTM OS would have done,

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:00.833
on real accounts.

00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:05.333
To take one engine live, an owner or
admin opens Settings, Safety, Engines,

00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:06.500
chooses Live, and confirms.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.800
The guardrails apply the moment
it flips, so check them first.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.500
The change goes to the audit
log, with who and when.

00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:14.333
Think of two locks on one door.

00:01:14.333 --> 00:01:17.233
The organization lock and the
engine lock must both be open.

00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:20.300
So you can keep outreach dry
while the organization is live,

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.033
and switching one engine
live loosens nothing else.

00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:24.267
Third, guardrails,

00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:27.367
which are limits the kernel enforces
for every engine and every agent.

00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:29.967
They cover emails per day, new
accounts contacted per week,

00:01:29.967 --> 00:01:32.733
and ad spend per day, above
which a person must approve.

00:01:32.733 --> 00:01:35.367
There's a send window in the
recipient's own timezone,

00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:37.900
with quiet hours that stay
right across daylight saving.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:40.900
You also choose the allowed channels
and set custom caps of your own.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.267
In the console: Settings, Safety,
Guardrails, and the Usage card.

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:47.100
Or ask your agent: "Show my
guardrails and today's usage".

00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:49.867
When a guardrail is hit, the
action is held, not dropped.

00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:53.100
An alert is raised, and the action
runs by itself once the limit clears.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.700
In the console: Settings,
Safety, held actions.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.200
Or ask your agent: "What is
being held right now, and why".

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.100
You can cancel any of it, and
work is never silently lost.

00:02:01.133 --> 00:02:05.167
Fourth, autonomy, where by default a
person approves each external action.

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:06.967
You can give an agent more room by scope,

00:02:06.967 --> 00:02:10.567
from the whole organization down to
one engine. Only a person sets it.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.800
The narrow way is a pre approval envelope.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.600
It covers one scope,
carries a budget, expires,

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.600
and is granted by a
person who can revoke it.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.000
Inside it, matching actions
can run without a fresh click.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.533
Outside it, a person still decides.
In the console: Setup, Rules card.

00:02:24.567 --> 00:02:26.600
One rule no setting can override.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.500
A first message to someone nobody at your

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.867
company has written to is
never sent automatically.

00:02:30.867 --> 00:02:34.000
Not with high autonomy, not
with an envelope, not by policy.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.100
A person always decides the first touch.

00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:39.000
Last, shadow accuracy, the
evidence for going live.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.100
It compares what an engine predicted
with what happened later, per engine.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.500
In the console: Settings, Safety, Engines.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.333
Or ask your agent: "Which engines
have shadow accuracy numbers,

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:47.933
and how do they look".

00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:50.867
With too little history, it says
so instead of showing a number.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.100
Before you flip any
engine, run four checks.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.000
Your guardrails hold numbers you'd defend,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.700
and a second person is named as approver.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.767
Shadow accuracy looks sane, and
the dry drafts are ones you'd send.

00:02:59.767 --> 00:03:00.867
Four yes answers, go.

00:03:00.867 --> 00:03:04.100
Pro move for week one: keep caps
tight, then read the held list,

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.800
which tells you which limit to widen.

00:03:05.833 --> 00:03:08.867
A sensible path is to keep
Dry Mode on while you build,

00:03:08.867 --> 00:03:10.200
and check shadow accuracy.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.833
Turn one engine live, inside tight
guardrails, with approvals on.

00:03:13.833 --> 00:03:15.467
Watch what gets held, then widen.

00:03:15.467 --> 00:03:17.767
Each step is a small
decision, on the record.

00:03:17.833 --> 00:03:21.367
You may be thinking: a safety switch
is only as good as who can flip it.

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:24.933
Only a person can switch Dry Mode. An
agent can read it and can't change it.

00:03:24.933 --> 00:03:27.767
Taking an engine live is
owner or admin, confirmed,

00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:29.100
and written to the audit log.

00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:31.767
To recap: two locks, and
the stricter one wins.

00:03:31.767 --> 00:03:33.667
Limits hold work and never drop it,

00:03:33.667 --> 00:03:36.533
and a first touch to a stranger
always waits for a person.

00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:40.333
You set how fast GTM OS goes live,
and every limit is enforced for you.

00:03:40.333 --> 00:03:42.767
Check your guardrails and keep
Dry Mode on while you build.

00:03:42.767 --> 00:03:45.700
Next: Why you can trust it,
the proof behind all of this.
